## A render-free core

Game state advances from the elapsed simulation time the game is handed,
independent of a canvas, of the frame loop that measured it, and of wall-clock
time. The dependency runs one way: the simulation reads nothing from the
renderer. Every rate is per second and integrated against the delta the frame
supplies, so an interval of game time covered as one frame and as sixty frames
advances `simTime` by the same amount and carries a flyer the same distance
along `x` at a constant `vx`. A step means the same thing whatever the frame
rate.

## What the runtime provides instead

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The overlay belongs to the runtime layer beneath the game, and the surface
carries no operation for it. That layer draws the overlay and toggles it from the
key `specs/controls.md` names.

The clock is the exception, because nothing outside this build owns it. The
surface is what takes the game off real time and advances it, through
`setAutoStep` and `advance` below.

Muting is the other. The runtime layer holds the mute bit, so `setMuted` below
sets it directly, and the HUD's `SOUND` control toggles that same bit.
{{else}}
The clock, the audio bus, and the overlay belong to the {{engine.name}} engine,
which documents them in the `engine/` directory at the root of this repository,
and the surface carries no operation for any of them. The engine advances the
game frame by frame and can take it off real time, it owns muting, and it draws
and toggles the debug overlay.

There is therefore no operation that sets muting: mute is reached the way a
player reaches it, through the HUD's `SOUND` control, and the snapshot reports
the result.
{{/if}}

What the overlay shows is Cascade's, under Diagnostics below.

The surface's pointer operations do not stand in for the player's pointer. They
feed the same input path it feeds, so a posed press and a player's press are the
same event to the game, and nothing is bypassed: the hit test, the grab rule, the
drop rule, and the double-click rule all run exactly as they do for a player.

## Identity

Every card and every flyer carries an `id`: a number, distinct among the entities
live at any moment, reported by `snapshot` and taken by every per-entity
operation. Two rules make an id findable without an assignment scheme:

- An entity added through this surface is appended to its pile or to the flyer
  list, so it is the last entry and its id is read from there.
- A card keeps its id for as long as it is on the table, across every move, turn,
  flip, and recycle, and a card that launches keeps it as a flyer.

## Addressing a pile

Every card operation names a pile with two scalars, `pile` and `index`:

| `pile` | `index` |
| --- | --- |
| `"stock"` | `0` |
| `"waste"` | `0` |
| `"foundation"` | `0` to `3` |
| `"tableau"` | `0` to `6` |

A pile is reported as an array ordered from its bottom card to its top card, so
the last element is the card on top. In a tableau column the last element is the
card drawn lowest on the table, which is the one a run is stacked onto.

## The operations

The surface carries `version` (`CASCADE_DEBUG_VERSION`, `1`), a plain number, and
the operations below. Values are plain numbers, strings, and booleans, and every
`x` and `y` the surface takes or reports is a card's top-left in the stage's
logical units, matching the anchors `specs/table.md` fixes.

An operation applies its effect every time it is called. It is the route a
driver has instead of a player, so what a player would have had to do first is
not its condition: which screen is showing, where the pointer has been, and
whether a control is drawn are how a hand reaches an effect, and this surface
reaches it from wherever the game stands. A pose reaches the value it names
whatever the game's own rules would have allowed, so a card is added to a pile
the deal would never have put it on and a flyer is put in flight on a table
nobody has won. A card operation carries out its own transaction from wherever
the game stands, and that transaction's own rules are the effect rather than a
gate on it: `deal()` deals from the title screen, `turnStock()` turns whatever
is on screen, and `move` and `autoMove` still report what the move rules
decided, because deciding that is what they are for.

What an operation never does is refuse quietly. A call the game has nothing to
act on — a `pile` and `index` naming no pile, a `count` that is not a whole
number, an `id` no card or flyer on the table carries — fails loudly rather
than returning with the state unchanged. A caller reads what changed from
`snapshot()`.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}

Each operation takes the current state, `DeepReadonly<CascadeState>`, as its
first parameter. A pose returns the next `CascadeState`, built from the one it
was handed, which it leaves as it was; a reading returns what it read. The
signatures below name the parameters that follow the state, so `setScreen(screen)`
below is `setScreen(state, screen)` on the surface and `snapshot()` is
`snapshot(state)`. `move` and `autoMove` both pose and report, so each returns the
pair `[nextState, verdict]` rather than a state alone.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "structured-2d")}}

Each operation takes only the parameters its signature names and acts on the live
game the moment it is called. A pose returns nothing; a reading returns what it
read and changes nothing. `move` and `autoMove` are the exception: each acts on
the live game and returns the verdict its row states.
{{/if}}

### The core

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `reset()` | Restores every declared field of the state to its title-screen value, listed below. |
| `snapshot()` | A pure read of the state, returned as the object under Snapshot shape below. It changes nothing. |
| `reconcile()` | Brings every value the surface reports into agreement with the table as it stands, advancing nothing. |
| `menuItemRect(index)` | The hit region of item `index` on the menu the current screen shows. It changes nothing. |

`reset` restores `screen` to `"title"`; sets `menuIndex` and `titleIndex` to `0`;
empties all thirteen piles and the waste's set memory; clears the run in hand,
the drop target, and the last press; puts the pointer at `(0, 0)` and up; turns
`autoFlip`, `winDetect`, `launching`, and `trailPainting` back on; sets
`launchClock` to `0`, `launched` to `0`, and `cascadeDone` to `false`; removes
every flyer; clears the painted layer and sets `trailStamps` to `0`; and sets
`simTime` to `0`.

`menuItemRect` returns `{ x, y, w, h }` in logical units, with `x` and `y` the
region's top-left corner and `w` and `h` its size: the region a pointer selects
that item from, as `specs/controls.md` states. It returns `null` on `won`, which
shows no menu, and when `index` names no item of the menu the current screen
shows.

`reset` takes no parameters. `muted` is left exactly as it stands, because
muting is a player preference the runtime owns.

`reconcile()` brings every value this surface reports into agreement with the
table as it now stands, without advancing anything. `wasteVisibleCount` follows
from the waste's set memory, a card's `color` from its suit, and `dropTarget`
from the run in hand and the pile it is held over, so after a pose they answer
for the table as it is now rather than as it was before.

A build that works those out at the read has nothing to do here and this call
changes nothing. A build that keeps any of them as a stored copy rewrites that
copy from what it is a copy of, through the same rule the game's own path uses.

It moves no clock and runs no system: `simTime` and `launchClock` are untouched,
no flyer moves, bounces, paints or retires, no card turns, no win is detected,
no cue is raised, and no draw is taken. It changes no screen, deals nothing, and
turns nothing. It moves nothing to make a reading agree: a flyer posed off the
stage stays where it was posed and reads as it is, and a run in hand is left in
hand whatever the pile beneath it now holds. `launched` and `trailStamps` are
figures the game has counted up rather than readings of the table as it stands,
so they are the cascade's and this call leaves them where it found them. Calling
it twice leaves the same state as calling it once.

It is never an error. It may be called on any screen, whether or not the game is
stepping itself, and before or after a `reset`.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}

`reconcile(state)` returns the next `CascadeState`, built from the one it was
handed, which it leaves as it was. A build that works every one of those readings
out at the call returns a state equal to the one it was given.
{{/if}}

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### The clock

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `setAutoStep(enabled)` | Takes the game off real time, and gives it back. |
| `advance(seconds, frames)` | Runs whole frames of game time immediately. |

`setAutoStep(false)` stops the frame loop advancing the game from the wall clock,
so the game changes only when `advance` says so. `setAutoStep(true)` returns it
to running itself, which is how a build starts and how it is played. Drawing is
unaffected either way: the loop keeps rendering, so the canvas shows the state
the most recent frame left. It changes no game state.

`advance(seconds, frames)` runs `frames` whole frames covering `seconds` of game
time, each worth `seconds / frames`, immediately and in order. `frames` defaults
to `1`. Each is a real frame, the same update the loop runs followed by a render,
so the game's own rules produce the result and the canvas reflects it.

### Muting

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `setMuted(muted)` | Sets whether sound is muted. |

It sets the runtime's mute bit, the same bit the HUD's `SOUND` control toggles
and the same bit `snapshot` reports as `muted`. It plays no cue and changes no
other field.

{{/if}}
### The screen and the menus

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `setScreen(screen)` | Sets the current screen: `"title"`, `"howto"`, `"playing"`, or `"won"`. |
| `setMenuIndex(index)` | Sets the selected item on the menu the current screen shows. |
| `setTitleIndex(index)` | Sets `titleIndex`, the title entry a return to the title restores. |

Each sets its own field alone. `setScreen` changes no other field, so a screen
posed this way leaves the table and the selection exactly as they stand, and the
screen it sets then behaves exactly as `specs/screens.md` states for it. Neither
`setMenuIndex` nor `setTitleIndex` touches the other's field, and neither
activates anything.

### The cards

A table is built one card at a time, so no operation takes a layout.

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `addCard(pile, index, suit, rank, faceUp)` | Adds one card of `suit` and `rank` to the top of the named pile, face-up or face-down as `faceUp` says. |
| `removeCard(id)` | Removes the card with that id from whichever pile holds it. |
| `setCardFaceUp(id, faceUp)` | Sets one card's face. |
| `clearPile(pile, index)` | Removes every card from the named pile. |
| `clearTable()` | Removes every card from all thirteen piles. |

`addCard` takes a `suit` of `"spades"`, `"hearts"`, `"diamonds"`, or `"clubs"`
and a `rank` from `1` for the Ace to `13` for the King. The card is appended, so
it is the pile's last entry, and it takes a fresh id the caller reads from the
snapshot. It touches no other pile and no other field, the waste's set memory
included.

`removeCard` leaves the rest of that pile in its order. Removing a card that is
on the waste takes it off the newest set that holds any, and a set left holding
nothing leaves the memory, which is the rule play itself follows.

`clearPile` leaves the other twelve piles standing. On the waste it also empties
the waste's set memory. It names a pile and touches nothing else, so it leaves
the run in hand and the drop target as they stand. `clearTable` empties all
thirteen piles and the waste's set memory, and it clears the run in hand and the
drop target with them, because a held run holds cards the clear has taken off the
table. It leaves the flyers, the painted layer, and every gate alone.

### The waste's sets

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `addWasteSet(count)` | Appends one set of `count` cards to the newest end of the waste's set memory. |
| `clearWasteSets()` | Empties the waste's set memory, leaving the cards on the waste standing. |

The set memory is the list `specs/stock.md` defines, and the cards on the waste
belong to those sets from the bottom up, so the oldest set holds the bottom-most
cards. `wasteVisibleCount` is the newest set's count, and `0` when the memory is
empty.

### The game's own events

Four operations are the game's own events rather than poses. Each routes through
exactly the code a player's gesture routes through and reports what the game's
own rules decided.

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `deal()` | Deals a fresh game, as `specs/deal.md` states. |
| `turnStock()` | Turns the stock, as `specs/stock.md` states. |
| `move(fromPile, fromIndex, fromRow, toPile, toIndex)` | Attempts a move and returns whether the rules accepted it. |
| `autoMove(pile, index)` | Sends the named pile's playable card home, and returns whether it went. |

`deal()` shuffles a full deck uniformly at random and deals it, as
`specs/deal.md` states. It replaces the contents of all thirteen piles, empties
the waste's set memory, sets `launched` to `0`, and clears the painted layer,
which is what a new deal does. It changes no other field, the screen included.

`turnStock()` turns the deal mode's turn count of cards from the stock onto the
waste, or recycles the whole waste back into the stock when the stock is empty.

`move` names its source with three scalars and its target with two. `fromRow` is
the index of the grabbed card within its pile, counted from the bottom, so `0` is
the pile's bottom-most card. The grabbed card and every card the pile holds after
it move together as a run, so in a column that is the grabbed card and every card
fanned below it on the table, and on a squared pile a `fromRow` naming the top
card takes that card alone. It returns `true` when the game's own rules accepted
the move and `false` when they refused it. An accepted move applies through the
same path a released drop uses, so a newly exposed column card turns and a
completed board wins. A refused move leaves the board unchanged.

`autoMove` sends the named pile's playable card to the foundation it belongs on
when that is legal, and does nothing otherwise. The playable card is the waste's
top card, or a column's lowest face-up card. A pile that holds no playable card
sends nothing, which covers an empty pile, a column whose lowest card is
face-down, and a foundation. It returns `true` when the card went home and
`false` when nothing moved.

### The pointer

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `pointerDown(x, y)` | Reports a press at a logical stage point. |
| `pointerMove(x, y)` | Reports a move to a logical stage point. |
| `pointerUp(x, y)` | Reports a release at a logical stage point. |

Each takes effect immediately, when it is called, rather than being sampled once
per frame. The press, the move, or the release is resolved before the call
returns rather than deferred, so a whole gesture can be driven from code without
advancing the game at all.

### The faculty gates

Four faculties are gated. Each gates one faculty and nothing else, each is on by
default and restored to on by `reset`, and each is reported by `snapshot`.

| Operation | Gates |
| --- | --- |
| `setAutoFlip(enabled)` | The turning face-up of a column's newly exposed lowest card. Off, a move that empties the face-up cards above a face-down card leaves that card face-down. |
| `setWinDetect(enabled)` | The check that all fifty-two cards are home and the move to the `won` screen. Off, a board completed to fifty-two stays on `playing`. |
| `setLaunching(enabled)` | The cascade's launch clock and the launching of the next card. Off, no further card leaves the foundations. Every card already in flight keeps flying, bouncing, painting, and retiring. |
| `setTrailPainting(enabled)` | The stamping of a card in flight onto the painted layer. Off, the layer takes no new stamp and `trailStamps` stops rising. The cards still fly and are still drawn at their positions. |

### The cascade

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `addFlyer(suit, rank, x, y, vx, vy)` | Adds one card in flight, its top-left at a logical stage position, with that velocity in logical units per second. |
| `setFlyerPosition(id, x, y)` | Sets that flyer's top-left. |
| `setFlyerVelocity(id, vx, vy)` | Sets that flyer's velocity. |
| `removeFlyer(id)` | Removes the flyer with that id. |
| `clearFlyers()` | Removes every flyer in flight. |
| `setLaunchClock(seconds)` | Sets the seconds accumulated toward the next launch. |
| `clearTrail()` | Clears the painted layer and sets `trailStamps` to `0`, leaving the flyers standing. |
| `drawLaunchVx()` | Performs one launch's `vx` draw and returns the signed value drawn. |

A flyer added by `addFlyer` is appended to the flight and takes a fresh id, and
it then flies, bounces, paints, and retires through the game's own cascade rules,
as `specs/victory.md` states.

`drawLaunchVx` performs exactly the draw a launch performs, as the launch table
in `specs/victory.md` states it: a magnitude drawn uniformly from
`[LAUNCH_VX_MIN, LAUNCH_VX_MAX]` with a sign chosen with equal probability. It
returns that signed speed, in logical units per second, and does nothing else:
no card launches, no flyer is added, and no declared field changes. Each call is
a fresh draw, independent of every other call and of every launch.

## Snapshot shape

`snapshot` returns exactly this object. Every field an operation can set is
present, so every operation is verifiable by setting a value and reading it back.

```js
{
  version: 1,
  screen: "title" | "howto" | "playing" | "won",
  menuIndex: <number>,         // the selected item on the current screen's menu
  titleIndex: <number>,        // the title menu's remembered selection
  dealMode: <string>,          // this build's DEAL_MODE
  turnCount: <number>,         // this build's TURN_COUNT
  dealModeLabel: <string>,     // this build's DEAL_MODE_LABEL
  muted: <boolean>,            // the game's copy of the runtime's mute bit

  // The four faculty gates.
  autoFlip: <boolean>,
  winDetect: <boolean>,
  launching: <boolean>,
  trailPainting: <boolean>,

  // The thirteen piles, each ordered bottom to top, so the last entry is the
  // card on top. A card is { id, suit, rank, color: "red" | "black", faceUp }.
  stock: [ <card>, ... ],
  waste: [ <card>, ... ],
  wasteSets: [ <number>, ... ],   // cards on each turned set, oldest first
  wasteVisibleCount: <number>,    // cards on the set the waste is showing
  foundations: [ <pile>, <pile>, <pile>, <pile> ],
  tableau: [ <pile>, <pile>, <pile>, <pile>, <pile>, <pile>, <pile> ],

  // The run currently in hand, or null when nothing is held.
  drag: null | {
    cards: [ <card>, ... ],       // cards[0] is the grabbed card
    fromPile: "waste" | "foundation" | "tableau",
    fromIndex: <number>,
    x: <number>, y: <number>,     // the top-left of cards[0]
  },
  // The pile a release would land the held run on, or null.
  dropTarget: null | { pile: "foundation" | "tableau", index: <number> },

  pointer: { x: <number>, y: <number>, down: <boolean> },
  // The most recent press, which the double-click rule is measured against.
  lastPress: null | { x: <number>, y: <number>, at: <number> },

  // The victory cascade.
  launchClock: <number>,        // seconds accumulated toward the next launch
  launched: <number>,           // cards the cascade has launched, 0 to 52
  flyers: [
    { id: <number>, suit: <string>, rank: <number>,
      x: <number>, y: <number>, vx: <number>, vy: <number> },
  ],
  cascadeDone: <boolean>,       // the cascade's own end flag
  trailStamps: <number>,        // stamps on the painted layer since it was cleared

  simTime: <number>,            // accumulated game time, in seconds
}
```

Every field is read straight off the game's state, so what the snapshot reports
is what the game holds. Six entries are derived rather than read off a field of
their own:

| Field | Derived from |
| --- | --- |
| `dealMode`, `turnCount`, `dealModeLabel` | The deal-mode figures `specs/stock.md` fixes |
| A card's `color` | Its suit, as `specs/deal.md` states |
| `wasteVisibleCount` | The newest entry of `wasteSets`, and `0` when `wasteSets` is empty |
| `dropTarget` | The run in hand, where it is held, and whether that pile accepts it |

A build is free to work any of these out at the read or to keep it as a stored
copy, and `reconcile()` is what brings a stored copy back into agreement after a
pose.

Five more need a note:

- `muted` is the game's copy of the runtime's mute bit, refreshed in every
  update, so the snapshot reports the bit the runtime holds.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
  `setMuted` sets that bit, and so does the HUD's `SOUND` control.
{{else}}
  No operation sets it; the HUD's `SOUND` control does.
{{/if}}
- `launched` is counted up by the cascade as it launches cards. It is not fifty-two
  less the cards on the foundations, so a posed flyer on a cleared table leaves it
  where it stands.
- `cascadeDone` is the flag the cascade's own end test sets when every card has
  launched and none is in flight. Posed flyers retiring on a cleared table do not
  set it.
- `lastPress` is written by the pointer path rather than posed, and it is
  reported here so the double-click rule is observable. `reset` clears it.
- `simTime` accumulates every update's delta, whatever the screen.

`screen` being `"won"` is the whole of the fact that the game is won, and the
snapshot reports no second field for it.

## Diagnostics

The debug overlay is read-only and shows the values the game registers with it as
diagnostic sources. Register at least:

- the current `screen` and the deal mode;
- how many cards are in the stock, on the waste, and on each of the four
  foundations;
- how many cards are in each of the seven columns;
- whether a run is in hand and how many cards it holds;
- while a cascade runs, how many cards have launched and how many are in flight.

Keep each one short enough to read on a line, and keep every source a pure read,
so watching the overlay leaves the game exactly as it is.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The overlay is part of the runtime layer you write. It draws the registered
values, it is shown and hidden by the key `specs/controls.md` names, it is off
when the game starts, and it reads the game without changing it.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Registering those values is the whole of Cascade's part, through
`InitApi.diagnostics`. Each source is called with the state current at the read,
as `DeepReadonly<CascadeState>`, and reads off that argument. Drawing the panel,
toggling it, and keeping it read-only are the engine's.
{{else}}
Registering those values is the whole of Cascade's part, through
`world.diagnostics` in the game mode's `beginPlay`. Each source is a function of
no arguments, called at each read; read the live state at the call, through the
world the mode holds, so the panel reports the frame being drawn. Drawing the
panel and toggling it are the engine's.
{{/if}}
